---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/siteUrl';
import '../../styles/docs-prose.css';
---

<Layout title="Local Plugins — Varve Docs" description="Install, review, run, stop, and develop local Varve plugin packages in the source-build preview.">
  <div class="container-custom">
    <div class="docs-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Documentation</a> / Local plugins</nav>
      <h1 class="site-page-title">Local plugins</h1>
      <p class="docs-intro">The current source-build preview accepts explicit local <code>.varveplugin</code> packages. It runs selected-layer analysis and previews batch renames. The first public API is deliberately small; see <a href={sitePath('/features/plugins')}>the feature overview</a> for its boundaries.</p>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Install and find a plugin</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>In an open design, choose <strong>File → Manage Plugins</strong>, or search for <strong>Manage Plugins</strong> in the command palette.</li>
          <li>Choose a local <code>.varveplugin</code> file. Review its package identity, requested access, and checksum.</li>
          <li>Grant required access and choose <strong>Install and enable</strong>, or install disabled and decide later.</li>
          <li>Use its command in the manager, or find a declared contextual panel in the Properties Inspector when a layer is selected.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The publisher name is supplied by the file itself. A checksum shows which bytes were installed; it does not authenticate the publisher. A design file cannot install or activate a plugin for you.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Access, previews, and recovery</h2>
        <p><strong>Read the current selection</strong> provides a bounded snapshot of selected layer names, kinds, lock state, and a few style counts. <strong>Change the open document</strong> permits a reviewed rename proposal to be applied. Neither permission grants file, network, clipboard, native command, or whole-document access.</p>
        <p>Use <strong>Stop</strong> to terminate a running command. Revoking access, disabling, updating, or removing a plugin invalidates pending results. If a plugin fails, its diagnostic stays in the manager until you choose Retry. A preview becomes stale when the document or selection changes; run it again before applying.</p>
        <p>Removing the package removes its commands and panels. Names already applied remain ordinary editable Varve artwork and can be saved or exported without the plugin. Undo committed edits while their document history is available. Plugin packages and permission grants stay in the local application profile, not in shared design files.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Build the two examples</h2>
        <p>The repository contains <a href="https://github.com/K-Arthur/varve/tree/master/examples/plugins">Selection Style Readiness and Number Selected Layers source, manifests, and a package builder</a>. With Rust's <code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code> target installed, run <code>node examples/plugins/build.mjs</code> from the repository root. The builder performs a real WebAssembly ABI smoke test and writes local packages in <code>examples/plugins/dist/</code>. Install those files through the manager; installation itself never runs Cargo, npm scripts, or a package setup command.</p>
        <p>The v1 package holds exactly <code>manifest.json</code> and <code>module.wasm</code>. Guest code imports only <code>env.memory</code> and exports <code>alloc</code>, <code>run</code>, and <code>result_len</code>. Input and output are bounded UTF-8 JSON. The examples use the typed <code>varve-plugin-sdk</code> Rust crate. Run <code>node --experimental-strip-types scripts/plugins/validate.mjs &lt;file.varveplugin&gt;</code> for a static package check before installation. Consult <a href="https://github.com/K-Arthur/varve/blob/master/docs/architecture/plugin-system.md">the API and threat-model contract</a> before building a package. Updates require selecting a newer local package and reviewing access again; there is no remote catalog or automatic update feed.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Current limits</h2>
        <p>Supported commands are selected-layer analysis and undoable renaming. Arbitrary HTML panels, custom nodes, background listeners, external network calls, local files, private plugin storage, native binaries, and a dependency resolver are not in API v1. Browser automation checks the user workflow; packaged desktop WebView testing on every operating system remains separate validation work.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .docs-page { max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 0 5rem; }
  .breadcrumb { margin-bottom: 2rem; color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: var(--type-interface-caption-size); }
  .breadcrumb a { color: var(--text-link); }
  .docs-section { margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }
  .docs-section p, .docs-section li { line-height: 1.75; }
  .docs-section code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
</style>
